        <h2>Twennie’s Proposal Win Strategy AI Companion</h2>

        <p class="feature-week-byline">
          How to Teach Your AI to Conduct a Candid, Evidence-Based Proposal Review
        </p>

        <div class="feature-week-author">
          <img
            src="/images/michele2.png"
            alt="Michele Rochon-Wood"
            class="feature-week-author-image"
          >

          <div class="feature-week-author-details">
            <div class="feature-week-author-label">
              AUTHOR
            </div>

            <div class="feature-week-author-name">
              Michele Rochon-Wood
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Artificial intelligence has become remarkably good at generating
          proposal content. What many professionals have not yet discovered is
          that AI can be even more valuable as a proposal reviewer.
        </p>

        <p>
          Unfortunately, a generic instruction such as
          <em>review this proposal</em> usually produces a generic response.
          The AI may comment on sentence length, grammar, passive voice, or
          formatting without examining whether the proposal presents a strong
          competitive argument.
        </p>

        <div class="feature-week-pullquote feature-week-animate">
          <p>
            Before AI can evaluate a proposal like an experienced strategist,
            someone has to teach it how.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>A Training Manual for Your AI</h3>

        <p>
          The Proposal Win Strategy AI Companion is not another chatbot or AI
          platform. It is an editable instruction set that teaches ChatGPT,
          Microsoft Copilot, Claude, Gemini, Fuel iX, and similar systems how
          to evaluate a technical proposal using Twennie’s
          <strong>Ten Steps to a Win Theme</strong> methodology.
        </p>

        <p>
          Think of the Companion as an onboarding manual for a new proposal
          manager. You would not hand a newly hired employee a proposal and
          simply ask what they thought. You would explain your review process,
          describe what matters to the client, and teach them how to recognize
          strong evidence and genuine differentiation.
        </p>

        <p>
          The AI Companion provides that same kind of training to artificial
          intelligence. Instead of writing a new prompt for every proposal,
          you can create a dedicated proposal evaluation assistant and teach
          it the methodology once.
        </p>

        <h3>Ten Sections of Strategic Evaluation</h3>

        <p>
          The Companion instructs the AI to evaluate the proposal in ten
          separate sections. Each section examines one part of the proposal’s
          competitive argument.
        </p>

        <p>
          The AI evaluates whether the proposal understands the client,
          understands the project, recognizes the issues, identifies critical
          success factors, and addresses important gaps in the proposal team’s
          knowledge.
        </p>

        <p>
          It then evaluates the features of the firm’s offer, the benefits
          those features create, the evidence that the promised benefits have
          been delivered before, and whether that evidence truly differentiates
          the firm from its competitors.
        </p>

        <p>
          Finally, it determines whether all of those elements have been woven
          together into one clear proposal story.
        </p>

        <div class="feature-week-pullquote feature-week-animate">
          <p>
            The quality of a proposal depends more on the quality of its
            thinking than the quality of its grammar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Build a Dedicated Proposal Evaluation Assistant</h3>

        <p>
          The accompanying video shows you how to create a reusable proposal
          evaluation assistant using the AI platform available to you.
        </p>

        <p>
          In ChatGPT, you can create a Custom GPT and upload the Companion as a
          permanent knowledge document. In Claude, you can create a Project
          that retains the Companion between reviews. Microsoft Copilot users
          can build an assistant within the Microsoft environment, while
          Google Workspace users can use Gemini with documents stored in
          Google Drive.
        </p>

        <p>
          Larger firms may use enterprise AI systems such as Fuel iX, which
          provide controlled access to several AI models through one secure
          organizational environment. The exact setup differs by platform, but
          the central process remains the same: create a dedicated assistant,
          provide the evaluation framework, and use that assistant consistently.
        </p>

        <h3>Give the AI Enough Information</h3>

        <p>
          AI can only evaluate the information available to it. Uploading the
          draft proposal alone may produce useful observations, but it will not
          allow the system to determine whether important client knowledge,
          project information, or competitive evidence was omitted.
        </p>

        <p>
          For a stronger review, provide the Request for Proposal, client
          meeting notes, site visit observations, pursuit strategy documents,
          proposal planning outputs, project profiles, lessons learned,
          proposal debriefs, and competitor intelligence whenever those
          materials are available.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Companion includes a section identifying which supporting
          documents are most valuable for each of its ten evaluation stages.
          These recommendations also provide proposal teams with a useful
          checklist for gathering and managing pursuit intelligence.
        </p>

        <div class="feature-week-pullquote feature-week-animate">
          <p>
            AI cannot recognize knowledge that your proposal team never
            collected or provided.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Receive a Candid Blue Team Review</h3>

        <p>
          Once the Companion, proposal, and supporting documents have been
          uploaded, the AI conducts a structured Blue Team review.
        </p>

        <p>
          For every section, it provides a score, identifies evidence found in
          the proposal, highlights missing evidence, explains the competitive
          risks created by weaknesses, and recommends practical improvements.
          It also assigns a confidence level so the proposal team can see
          whether its conclusions are based on strong evidence or limited
          information.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Companion explicitly instructs the AI not to reassure the team
          that the proposal is good enough. Its job is to conduct a candid
          assessment of the submission’s chances in a competitive marketplace
          and identify improvements while there is still time to make them.
        </p>

        <h3>Think Before You Rewrite</h3>

        <p>
          One of the most important instructions in the Companion appears on
          its final page: do not rewrite the proposal immediately.
        </p>

        <p>
          Proposal teams should review the evaluation together, discuss the
          findings, decide which recommendations are valid, and agree on the
          strategy before asking AI to produce replacement text.
        </p>

        <p>
          AI should function first as a reviewer and strategic advisor. Only
          after the proposal team has exercised its professional judgment
          should the system be asked to strengthen an executive summary,
          revise a section, improve a win theme, or suggest better headings.
        </p>

        <div class="feature-week-pullquote feature-week-animate">
          <p>
            Use AI to improve the thinking behind the proposal before using it
            to improve the words.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Edit the Companion for Your Firm</h3>

        <p>
          The Proposal Win Strategy AI Companion is provided as an editable
          template. Firms can adapt the instructions, terminology, scoring
          system, and evaluation standards to reflect their own proposal
          process.
        </p>

        <p>
          Over time, the assistant can also be given additional organizational
          knowledge, including proposal standards, writing guidelines,
          project databases, debrief records, lessons learned, and established
          review criteria.
        </p>

        <p>
          This allows a firm to build a proposal evaluator that applies the
          same standards across offices, teams, and pursuits while still
          supporting the judgment of experienced proposal professionals.
        </p>

        <h3>The First Twennie AI Companion</h3>

        <p>
          The Proposal Win Strategy Evaluator is the first in a planned
          collection of Twennie AI Companions. Future resources may help AI
          evaluate proposal readability, project risk, scope management,
          business development strategy, client relationships, leadership,
          communication, and other areas of technical consulting work.
        </p>

        <p>
          Eventually, these methodologies will become part of Twennie’s own
          integrated AI. Instead of teaching another AI platform how to conduct
          the evaluation, members will be able to upload their proposals and
          supporting documents directly into Twennie.
        </p>

        <p>
          The methodology will remain the same. Only the delivery platform
          will change.
        </p>

        <p>
          Twennie’s goal is not to replace proposal managers or professional
          judgment. It is to give technical professionals access to a
          thoughtful, disciplined, and consistent reviewer before their work
          reaches a client.
        </p>

